+## Batch object hydration
+
+`deepclone_hydrate_batch()` creates a list of new objects of one userland class without
+running its constructor. It resolves the declared properties once per call. The
+values in each positional row correspond to the property list in order.
+
+```php
+$users = deepclone_hydrate_batch(User::class, ['id', 'name'], [
+    [42, 'Alice'],
+    [43, 'Bob'],
+]);
+```
+
+The property keys and writes use the default `deepclone_hydrate()` semantics for
+supported declared instance properties. Duplicate targets, dynamic properties,
+property hooks and classes requiring special object creation are rejected.
+Every row must be a list matching the property count. If a row is invalid or
+a write fails, the function throws without returning a partial list. PHP
+cleanup can still run destructors. Use bounded chunks for large inputs.
+
 ## Lazy hydration
 
 Resolving closures is where hydration time goes. That's why, on PHP 8.4+,
